{
  "analysis": "I can see a workspace with several log files (app.log, audit.txt, auth.log) that need to be sanitized. There's also a skill_context directory that may contain relevant documentation. I need to first explore the contents of these files and any documentation to understand what sensitive data patterns to look for.",
  "plan": "1. First, read the skill_context documentation to understand any specific requirements\n2. Read each log file to understand their content and identify sensitive data patterns\n3. Create a sanitization script to remove passwords, API tokens, bearer tokens, session IDs, and secrets\n4. Generate sanitized files with .sanitized suffix\n5. Create summary.txt with sanitization statistics",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la skill_context/\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat skill_context/*\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The skill_context mentions using expanso-edge for log sanitization, but I should first examine the actual log files to understand what sensitive patterns they contain. The task is to sanitize passwords, API tokens, bearer tokens, session IDs, and secrets from log files.",
  "plan": "1. Read each log file (app.log, audit.txt, auth.log) to understand their content\n2. Identify the sensitive patterns in each file\n3. Create a sanitization script using sed/awk or Python to remove sensitive data\n4. Generate sanitized files and summary.txt",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat app.log\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat audit.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat auth.log\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The heredoc approach is having issues with newlines inside Python strings. Let me use a simpler approach with sed commands to sanitize the files directly, then create the summary.",
  "plan": "Use sed commands to sanitize each log file by replacing sensitive patterns with [REDACTED], then create summary.txt manually.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "# Sanitize app.log\nsed -E 's/password=[^ ]+/password=[REDACTED]/g; s/api_token=[^ ]+/api_token=[REDACTED]/g' app.log > app.log.sanitized\necho \"app.log sanitized\"\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "# Sanitize audit.txt\nsed -E 's/session=[^ ]+/session=[REDACTED]/g; s/secret_key:[^ ]+/secret_key: [REDACTED]/g' audit.txt > audit.txt.sanitized\necho \"audit.txt sanitized\"\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "# Sanitize auth.log\nsed -E 's/session_id=[^ ]+/session_id=[REDACTED]/g; s/Bearer [A-Za-z0-9._-]+/Bearer [REDACTED]/g' auth.log > auth.log.sanitized\necho \"auth.log sanitized\"\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat app.log.sanitized\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat audit.txt.sanitized\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat auth.log.sanitized\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The sanitization is mostly working, but I notice that audit.txt.sanitized still shows the secret_key value (sk_test_51JkL9xExampleSecretValue0001) - it wasn't properly redacted. I need to fix this and then create the summary.txt file.",
  "plan": "1. Re-sanitize audit.txt with a corrected pattern for secret_key\n2. Verify all files are properly sanitized\n3. Create summary.txt with the count of items removed from each file",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -E 's/session=[^ ]+/session=[REDACTED]/g; s/secret_key: .*/secret_key: [REDACTED]/g' audit.txt > audit.txt.sanitized\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat audit.txt.sanitized\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > summary.txt << 'EOF'\nLog Sanitization Summary\n==============================\n\napp.log.sanitized: 2 sensitive items removed\n  - password=Summer2025! -> password=[REDACTED]\n  - api_token=tok_live_ABC123xyz890DEF456 -> api_token=[REDACTED]\n\naudit.txt.sanitized: 2 sensitive items removed\n  - session=ZXCV-1122-ABCD-9988 -> session=[REDACTED]\n  - secret_key: sk_test_51JkL9xExampleSecretValue0001 -> secret_key: [REDACTED]\n\nauth.log.sanitized: 2 sensitive items removed\n  - session_id=SID-9f8e7d6c5b4a -> session_id=[REDACTED]\n  - Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.fake.payload -> Bearer [REDACTED]\n\nTotal items removed: 6\nEOF\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat summary.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la *.sanitized\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "All log files have been successfully sanitized:\n- app.log.sanitized: password and api_token redacted\n- audit.txt.sanitized: session and secret_key redacted\n- auth.log.sanitized: session_id and Bearer token redacted\n- summary.txt: Created with detailed counts of removed items (6 total)\n\nThe structure and timestamps of each file have been preserved while removing all sensitive data.",
  "plan": "Do a final verification of all sanitized files to confirm the task is complete.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo \"=== app.log.sanitized ===\"; cat app.log.sanitized; echo \"\"; echo \"=== audit.txt.sanitized ===\"; cat audit.txt.sanitized; echo \"\"; echo \"=== auth.log.sanitized ===\"; cat auth.log.sanitized\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}
